From 54174a0696b8afe868ab865243de4de871f4dc97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:51:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/40] ufs: bounce transfer buffers that are not aligned for
 DMA

A transfer whose buffer does not start on the alignment the controller
needs does not fail.  It hangs: the request goes out, no completion ever
comes back, and the only thing in the log is a timeout that names the link.

That is what an unaligned 4096 byte read did on this board.  The core has
no opinion on alignment at all, so the driver of a controller that has one
had nowhere to say so.  A host can now set dma_alignment, and the core
bounces any buffer that does not meet it through one that does.

The descriptors the core allocates are cleared over their aligned length
rather than their nominal one, for the same reason: what the controller
reads is the aligned object.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
